<p>If your dog ate tinsel, call your veterinarian now, even if your dog seems completely normal. Tinsel is not a poison, but a swallowed strand can catch inside the gut and slowly cut the intestines, which can become a surgical emergency. Do not make your dog vomit and never pull on a strand you can see. A vet can tell you whether to come in.</p>

<div class="pz-quick-steps" style="background:#FFF4EC;border-left:4px solid #FF6B1A;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:16px 20px;margin:24px 0">
<p><strong>My dog just swallowed tinsel: quick steps</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Remove the rest of the tinsel</strong> and keep your dog away from the tree. Note roughly how much is missing and when it was eaten.</li>
<li><strong>Do not pull</strong> any strand hanging from the mouth or bottom, do not make your dog vomit, and do not give food or home remedies to “push it through”.</li>
<li><strong>Call your vet or an emergency clinic now.</strong> For help deciding, call the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center at (888) 426-4435 or Pet Poison Helpline at (855) 764-7661 (24 hours; a fee may apply).</li>
