<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Tinsel is usually plastic or metallic film. Pet Poison Helpline says it is not “poisonous” itself, but it can become a linear foreign body when eaten.</li>
<li>The ASPCA warns swallowed tinsel can cause severe vomiting, a blocked digestive tract and dehydration, and may need surgery.</li>
<li>Some pets show no signs at first, PetMD notes, so “seems fine” does not mean safe.</li>
<li>Never pull a strand from the mouth or bottom. The Merck Veterinary Manual warns pulling an anchored string can perforate the gut.</li>
<li>The Merck Veterinary Manual says cats are even more likely than dogs to develop a blockage from string-like objects.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Is Tinsel Toxic to Dogs?</h2>
<p>Tinsel is generally not toxic to dogs, but it is dangerous. Tinsel is a holiday decoration made of thin strands of shiny plastic or metallic film, sold loose or as rope-style garland. Dogs cannot digest it, so the risk is physical: strands can anchor and block or tear the gut.</p>
<p>Pet Poison Helpline says: “While tinsel itself is not ‘poisonous,’ it can result in a linear foreign body when eaten.” The AKC agrees it “can cause severe damage to a dog’s intestinal tract if swallowed”. One chemical exception: Pet Poison Helpline notes some tinsel and garland may contain zinc, which can cause vomiting, loss of appetite and pale gums from life-threatening anemia.</p>

<h2>What Happens If a Dog Eats Tinsel?</h2>
<p>When a dog eats tinsel, the strands either pass, cause a blockage, or anchor and act as a linear foreign body. You cannot tell which at home.</p>

<h3>What is a linear foreign body?</h3>
<p>A linear foreign body is a long, thin object, such as string, yarn, ribbon or tinsel, that gets stuck in the digestive tract. The Merck Veterinary Manual says one end is typically caught at the base of the tongue or in the stomach, causing the intestines to bunch up while the material saws through the intestinal wall.</p>
<p>VCA names intestinal perforation (a tear in the bowel) as the most significant complication, which can lead to peritonitis, a life-threatening infection of the belly lining. Cornell University’s Riney Canine Health Center adds that string and ribbon “can cause more severe damage” than other foreign bodies.</p>

<h2>Dog Ate Tinsel: What to Do Right Now</h2>
<p>Call your veterinarian right away and describe what was eaten, how much and when. Pet Poison Helpline and PetMD both advise contacting a vet or a poison helpline if a pet eats any tinsel. Bring a sample or the packaging so your vet can see whether it is metallic. Our <a href="/tools/dog-first-aid-guide/">dog first aid guide</a> covers what to have ready.</p>

<h3>Will my dog be OK if he ate tinsel?</h3>
<p>Many dogs that swallow a small amount are fine, but nobody can predict which ones will not be. PetMD says some pets show no signs at first and that, although larger dogs may pass tinsel, the outcome cannot be predicted. Cornell notes the best outcomes come when an obstruction is found and removed promptly, which is why calling early matters.</p>
<p>A dog that is eating and playing normally is good news, not an all-clear. Ask your vet which signs should bring you in.</p>

<h3>Should I make my dog throw up?</h3>
<p>No, not on your own. Pet Poison Helpline advises contacting a veterinarian or the helpline before trying to induce vomiting in a dog, and lists injury from vomiting sharp objects among the risks. Your vet will decide whether removal by vomiting is safe for your dog.</p>

<h3>What if tinsel is sticking out of my dog’s bottom or mouth?</h3>
<p>Do not pull it, and call your vet. The Merck Veterinary Manual warns that a linear foreign body must never be pulled, because if it is embedded in tissue, pulling can perforate the gut. VCA gives the same advice when tinsel or ribbon is coming out of the anus: “do not pull!” A vet can check whether the other end is still anchored.</p>

<h2>Signs of an Intestinal Blockage in Dogs</h2>
<p>Signs of an intestinal blockage in dogs include vomiting, not eating, belly pain, diarrhea, dehydration and lethargy, according to Cornell and the Merck Veterinary Manual. PetMD adds straining to poop or not pooping at all. Any of these after a dog swallowed tinsel need a vet the same day, and some need an emergency vet now (see the table).</p>

