<p>Signs from Cornell, Merck, PetMD, VCA and Pet Poison Helpline (choking and coughing: PetMD; pawing at the mouth: VCA; pale gums: zinc-coated tinsel). Our checklist of <a href="/blog/signs-your-pet-is-sick/">signs your pet is sick</a> helps you describe what you see when you call.</p>

<h3>Will tinsel pass through a dog?</h3>
<p>Sometimes, but you cannot count on it. The Merck Veterinary Manual says some foreign bodies, especially small, smooth ones, may pass without surgery. Tinsel is neither, and string-like objects carry a higher perforation risk, so only your vet can judge whether waiting is reasonable.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/pet-stomach-upset-guide/">pet stomach upset guide</a> covers everyday tummy trouble, but after tinsel, treat vomiting as a possible blockage until a vet says otherwise.</p>

<h2>What Will the Vet Do?</h2>
<p>A vet will usually start with a full exam, including under the tongue, then imaging. Cornell lists X-rays or ultrasound, plus bloodwork. VCA notes X-rays may show a “string-of-pearls” pattern where the intestines have bunched up.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> for a small amount and a well dog, your vet may suggest supportive care and close watching. Cornell says surgery is needed if the object does not pass.</li>
<li><strong>Endoscopy:</strong> a camera passed through the mouth, mainly useful while the object is still in the stomach (Cornell, Merck).</li>
<li><strong>Surgery:</strong> Cornell says most foreign body obstructions need surgery, which may include opening the stomach or intestine and removing damaged bowel.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Puppy Ate Tinsel? Why Young Dogs Are at Higher Risk</h2>
<p>VCA warns that ribbons and strings used to wrap gifts are hazardous “especially to young puppies”, and that teething puppies can bite through the insulation on light and extension cords. If your puppy ate tinsel, follow the steps above.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/new-puppy-checklist-everything-you-need/">new puppy checklist</a> covers the chew toys and gates that make a first Christmas easier.</p>

<h2>Cat Ate Tinsel? Cats Are at Even Higher Risk</h2>
<p>If your cat ate tinsel, treat it as an emergency. The Merck Veterinary Manual says cats are more likely than dogs to develop an intestinal obstruction from a linear foreign body, and PetMD considers any amount of tinsel in a cat an emergency because of their small digestive tract. Pet Poison Helpline’s advice: “If you own a cat, toss the tinsel!”</p>
<p>VCA lists vomiting, refusing food, dehydration and lethargy as common signs, and some cats paw at the mouth. Pet Poison Helpline says home vomiting is not safe or effective for cats. Our <a href="/tools/cat-vomiting-guide/">cat vomiting guide</a> explains other causes, and if your cat climbs the tree, read <a href="/blog/how-to-cat-proof-a-christmas-tree/">how to cat-proof a Christmas tree</a>.</p>

<h2>Other Christmas Decorations That Can Hurt Dogs</h2>
<p>If your dog ate tinsel garland, ribbon or string, the same linear foreign body danger applies. Other decorations bring cuts, burns, electric shock or poisoning.</p>

