<h2>How Do I Stop My Dog Eating Tinsel?</h2>
<p>The surest way to stop a dog eating tinsel is not to use it. The ASPCA suggests decorating with something other than tinsel, and an AKC holiday article says, “Just say no to tinsel.” If you keep it, make it hard to reach and supervise your dog near the tree.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pick rope-style garland over loose strands</strong> and hang it high; PetMD says rope tinsel is harder to swallow.</li>
<li><strong>Decorate the lower branches lightly.</strong> PetMD advises avoiding decorations and lights on low branches.</li>
<li><strong>Block access</strong> with a gate or pen around the tree, and keep your dog out of the room when nobody is watching, especially overnight (PetMD).</li>
<li><strong>Clear up right away.</strong> The AKC recommends disposing of wrapping paper, bows, yarn and curling ribbon right after gifts are opened.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum fallen strands</strong> and teach “leave it”; Cornell recommends training dogs not to scavenge.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/pet-proof-home-guide/">room-by-room pet-proofing guide</a> has more ways to make the house safer year-round.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>How long does it take for a dog to pass tinsel?</h3>
<p>There is no reliable timeframe, and a dog may never pass an anchored strand. The Merck Veterinary Manual notes that when a vet chooses to monitor a small, smooth foreign body, failure to pass within 36 to 48 hours is a reason for surgery. Tinsel is a higher-risk object, so let your vet set the plan.</p>

<h3>Can a dog die from eating tinsel?</h3>
<p>Yes, in serious cases. The AKC says tinsel, “like any long, stringy item, can prove deadly if ingested.” In 2019, Fox News reported that Benji, an 8-year-old German Shepherd in Manchester, England, had emergency surgery after X-rays showed roughly three feet of tinsel inside him, and was recovering.</p>

<h3>Why do some sites call tinsel a poison?</h3>
<p>Because the term is used loosely. PetMD headlines its guide “Tinsel Poisoning in Dogs and Cats”, yet notes tinsel is not often made from a toxic substance. The main danger is a physical blockage, not a poison. The exception is zinc-coated tinsel, which Pet Poison Helpline says can cause anemia, so tell your vet if the tinsel looked metallic.</p>

<h3>Does a dog that ate tinsel always need surgery?</h3>
<p>No. Some dogs pass small amounts, and Cornell says a vet may try supportive care with monitoring when an object is not causing a full blockage. If it does not pass, is anchored, or sits beyond the stomach where endoscopy cannot reach, surgery is usually needed.</p>

<h3>Is rope tinsel garland safer than loose strands?</h3>
<p>Somewhat safer, not safe. PetMD says rope-style tinsel is harder to swallow than single strands, but a dog that chews a garland can still swallow pieces. Hang it well out of reach, or skip it.</p>

<h3>Can I give my dog something at home to help tinsel pass?</h3>
<p>No. Do not give food, oils, laxatives or any home remedy unless your vet tells you to; an anchored strand needs a vet to remove it.</p>

<h3>Why does my dog keep eating tinsel and other decorations?</h3>
<p>Sparkly, light-catching tinsel looks like a toy, and VCA notes young puppies delight in playing with and chewing ribbons and strings. Offer safe chew toys, supervise near the tree, and ask your vet if your dog often swallows non-food items.</p>

<h3>What should I tell the vet when I call?</h3>
<p>Say what your dog ate, roughly how much and when, whether it was metallic, your dog’s weight and age, and any signs so far, such as vomiting or not eating. Mention any visible strand and that you have not pulled it.</p>

